{
  "_source": "Worked out by hand from the rule written on NumberParser (STMCore) and parseNumber (Money.kt), with at most 2 fraction digits. 1: trim, then drop spaces, U+00A0, U+202F, € and EUR; anything else left over is invalid. 2: one sign marker at most: leading or trailing - or U+2212, or wrapping parentheses, mean negative; a leading + is ignored. 3: only digits, comma and point remain. 4: with both, the last one is the decimal separator and appears once, the other groups thousands. 5: one kind more than once groups thousands. 6: one kind once: a comma is always decimal; a point followed by exactly 3 digits groups thousands here because money holds fewer than 3 fraction digits (a field that holds 3 or more rejects it, see decimal_parse.json), otherwise it is decimal. 7: groups are 1 to 3 digits, the first not starting with 0, then exactly 3 each. 8: at most 2 digits after the decimal separator, more is invalid, never rounded; a trailing decimal separator with no digits after it is accepted and adds no fraction (12, is 12). 9: the integer part may be empty. 10: more than 15 integer digits is invalid. Amounts are integer cents; null means invalid.",
